Custom fonts have become a routine part of building for the web. The [2025 Web Almanac](https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2025/fonts#webfont-usage) found that roughly 88% of the pages surveyed requested web fonts. This website does too. While improving its performance, I noticed it was serving entire font files, including characters and weights it never used. That led me to investigate how much could be removed and what the tools actually kept.

## Why fonts contain more than a page needs

Fonts are designed to be used across many different websites and documents. They often include letters for multiple languages, along with punctuation and symbols. While operating systems use formats like TTF and OTF for fast, uncompressed access on local drives, WOFF2 is the standard for the web because it compresses that same font data losslessly for network transfer.

I want to use [Vercel's Geist Sans v1.7.2](https://vercel.com/font) as an example. At the time of writing, decompressing Geist[wght].woff2 yields 169.06 KB<sup id="fnref-1"><a href="#fn-1">1</a></sup>. WOFF2 uses [Brotli compression](https://www.w3.org/TR/WOFF2/#compressed-data-format) alongside font-specific transformations to bring the download down to 69.65 KB.

Font data is organized into sections called [font tables](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/otff#font-tables), each with a short identifier. Many of these tables revolve around storing and shaping glyphs.

A **glyph** is a shape a font uses to display text, such as a letter, punctuation mark, or symbol. Characters and glyphs aren't quite the same thing: the characters `f` and `i`, for example, can be drawn together as a single joined glyph.

Here are the largest tables in the Geist file and what they contain:

